{"authored" [{"question" "e-6996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/d1457393a0b919a208da95e0b819b50a83847d2a5dfbb9c4c81c4c457a2cd467.png)\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\n- $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-6996/review.json" "file_sha256" "b4a0f05c359069af15c5e865bf978c4e48e4e29659e68ad657574085760b7dfb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-6996/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-6996/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practice/canonical prompts now consistently state the intended containment criterion. Preserve original source prompts, accepted keys, decisions, grades, unchanged field/answer identities and historical question versions."} {"question" "e-6996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For this question, $R$ must contain every point of $D$. Extending the integrand by zero on $R\\setminus D$ then preserves the integral: the function equals the original integrand throughout $D$ and contributes zero outside it. The rectangles must therefore be checked for containment, as explicitly required in the prompt.\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/8e0d0d508d61352edf461f147cc561a527f6b3191e6c6b11d070eedf1e6572e3.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-6996/review.json" "file_sha256" "b4a0f05c359069af15c5e865bf978c4e48e4e29659e68ad657574085760b7dfb" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-6996/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "0f51fb6863ec7b809159c261cd87890fe636a8584879f0790bcfc97d8baec053" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-6996/verification.json" "basis" 1745 "rationale" "Make the source worked solution's explicit containing-region construction part of the study request. D subset R is sufficient for zero-extension integral equality; an equality for one chosen function does not in general require containment because cancellation can occur. q80266 decisively demonstrates the difference through the odd integrand xy: all original rectangles satisfy the literal equality, while only II and III contain D. This lesson's related practice/canonical prompts now consistently state the intended containment criterion. Preserve original source prompts, accepted keys, decisions, grades, unchanged field/answer identities and historical question versions."} {"question" "e-6996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/d1457393a0b919a208da95e0b819b50a83847d2a5dfbb9c4c81c4c457a2cd467.png)\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\n- $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/example-6996.json" "file_sha256" "780da8bd7ef315d02084244a16d8c10280648e0aa98efe78bf15346c51028ea9" "basis" 1742 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/8e0d0d508d61352edf461f147cc561a527f6b3191e6c6b11d070eedf1e6572e3.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct ans